sitions.\n\ncoloring\n\n2006-06-15\n\nMassimo Caboara, Sara Faridi, Peter Selinger\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nSimplicial cycles and the computation of simplicial trees\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe generalize the concept of a cycle from graphs to simplicial complexes. We show that a simplicial cycle is either a sequence of facets connected in the shape of a circle, or is a cone over such a structure. We show that a simplicial tree is a connected cycle-free simplicial complex, and use this characterization to produce an algorithm that checks in polynomial time whether a simplicial complex is a tree. We also present an efficient algorithm for checking whether a simplicial complex is grafted, and therefore Cohen-Macaulay.\n\ncohen-macaulay graph polynomial time sequence simplicial complex polynomial tree\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Selinger_P in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ASelinger_P+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{math/0606375, author = {Massimo Caboara and Sara Faridi and Peter Selinger}, title = {{S}implicial cycles and the computation of simplicial trees}, year = {2006}, eprint = {math/0606375}, note = {arXiv:math/0606375v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We show that a simplicial tree is a connected cycle-free simplicial complex, and use this characterization to produce an algorithm that checks in polynomial time whether a simplicial complex is a tree.\nWhere possible, we provide pointers to more rigorous treatments in the literature. Where we include results that have only been proved in special cases, we indicate this in the form of caveats.\n\n  * Simplicial cycles and the computation of simplicial trees\n\nMassimo Caboara, Sara Faridi, Peter Selinger\n\nJun 16 2006 math.AC math.CO arXiv:math/0606375v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{math/0606375, author = {Massimo Caboara and Sara Faridi and Pe